\f1\fs22 At around ten, Chanta was woken by buskers out in the street, squabbling over the better lyrics for a song. Sarah stayed face-planted in a pillow, dead to the world.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He got up to relieve himself. While spraying his morning\'92s especially vigorous stream around the toilet bowl, he amused himself, discovering just how many twinkling tones he could make. He thought of Sarah and, feeling virtuous, decided to wash his hands.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He never turned on the tap. Instead, a flicker in the mirror stuck his attention. He frowned, boggled, closed and opened his eyes (together, then one at a time), bared his teeth, turned left and right, looked up his nostrils, pulled monkey faces. The image in the mirror mimicked him, beat for beat. Impossible. He reached up and felt his face. No mistake\'97the mirror-mirror-on-the-wall was right: handsomest Chanta of them all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He giggled, did a rogue dance step, rolled up his singlet, and checked his chest and back. The mirror confirmed it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He\'92d never felt so good. Lungs full and pink, belly a veritable six-pack. No creaking bones, no sore teeth. He stood taller, he was sure; maybe his hairline had even recovered a bit.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The best? His burns had healed. Every last mark, gone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta felt invincible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What was this miracle? He was seconds from waking Sarah\'97he\'92d lifted a pillow and was ready to whack her\'97when she snored and snorted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Nah, not attractive,\'94 Chanta muttered. Suddenly his arms felt leaden, the pillow too heavy to bother.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But she was attractive\'97too attractive by far, Chanta admitted. Beautiful enough to steep him in longing that made his heart ache. He let her sleep and, with the unbounded puppy exuberance only the newly renewed can muster, needed to do something. Anything. So he marched, and the door opened, and everything in the hall seemed brighter, the stairwell less a disaster zone, the Layer outside less acidic, the sun\'92s rays somehow more attentive warming his skin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He needed a purpose. Something noble. Of course: Sarah deserved a coffee.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A simple enough mission. Except, in the Ghetto, \'93coffee\'94 regulation made sugar, half-and-half, and amphetamines all but interchangeable. Depending on the Elvi clone series, preferences could run to half a cup of half-and-half, 418 sugar cubes, and as much of Dr Nick\'92s Mix as you liked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta wisely skipped the extras, especially the half-and-half. He wandered, on a quest for a simple yoghurt frappuccino with raw sugar, caramel drizzle, and blueberry topping.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He found none. The more intent he grew, the more the Ghetto eluded him. By the time Chanta noticed, he\'92d zigzagged out of the Ghetto altogether.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That\'92s when four cat-sized hands\'97belonging to two wise guys\'97grabbed his shoulders and knees, and bundled him into the back of a shiny black suburban.
